        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "css-select does not support the :lang() pseudo-class: compiling a selector that uses it throws 'Unknown pseudo-class :lang'. Add it as a filter in src/pseudo-selectors/filters.ts, following Selectors Level 4. The argument is a comma-separated list of language ranges; each range is trimmed, an empty entry is ignored, surrounding single or double quotes are removed, and matching is case-insensitive. An element's language is the value of its own xml:lang attribute if it has one, else its own lang attribute, else the same lookup on its nearest element ancestor. An element with no such attribute on itself or any ancestor, or whose nearest value is the empty string (lang=\"\"), matches only an empty range (such as :lang(\"\")). A range matches a language tag by RFC 4647 extended filtering over the hyphen-separated subtags: the first subtags must be equal unless the range's first subtag is '*'; then each later range subtag that is not '*' must be found, in order, among the tag's remaining subtags, where tag subtags of two or more characters may be skipped but reaching a one-character (singleton) subtag fails the match; later '*' range subtags are skipped. So :lang(en) matches elements with lang=\"en\" or lang=\"en-US\" (and their descendants without their own lang) but not lang=\"eng\"; :lang(de-DE) matches \"de-DE\", \"de-Latn-DE\" and \"de-Latn-DE-1996\"; :lang(\\*-CH) matches \"de-CH\" and \"fr-CH\" but not \"fr-FR\"; :lang(en, fr) matches either language. The filter composes with the rest of a compound selector like the other filters (for example p:lang(en)). Nothing else changes. Change only src/pseudo-selectors/filters.ts; do not edit tests. The full check (npm test: vitest, then eslint, biome and tsc) must pass.\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): npm run build && node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/."
      ],
